IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pidq-dates`

-- Public Interest Disclosure Act 2010 (Qld). An Act "to facilitate the disclosure, in the public interest, of
-- information about wrongdoing in the public sector and to provide protection for those who make disclosures".
--
-- This encoding takes the provisions a whistleblower meets, and in particular the claims machinery a person who suffers
-- a reprisal meets: what may be disclosed and by whom (ss 12, 13, 19), to whom (ss 14-16, 20) and how (s 17); what the
-- receiving entity must or may do and tell (ss 30, 32) and the 28-day review (s 30(3)); the journalist route and its
-- 6 months (s 20); the discloser's protections (ss 36-39) and exposure (s 66, with ss 67, 69); what a reprisal is
-- (ss 40, 45, schedule 4 "detriment"); the reprisal offence (s 41); damages in tort (s 42) and against the employer
-- (s 43); a complaint under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 instead (s 44); appeals (s 46), relocation (s 47) and
-- injunctions (ss 48-50); and the confidentiality of the discloser's identity (s 65).
--
-- SEAT: the discloser -- a person (a public officer, a GOC employee, or any member of the public) who has information
-- about wrongdoing in the Queensland public sector, has disclosed it or is thinking of doing so, and may have suffered a
-- reprisal for it and want damages or another remedy. "Can I report this? To whom? Will they tell me what happened? Can
-- I go to the press? Am I protected? Was what they did to me a reprisal? Can I sue, and whom? Can I get an injunction or
-- be moved?" Every judgment the Act gives an entity, a manager, the industrial relations commission or a court
-- ("reasonable", "considers", "satisfied", "substantial") is asked as an input, never decided.
--
-- Edition: Public Interest Disclosure Act 2010 (Qld), the version in force from 30 August 2024, current on 6 November
-- 2026, deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/act-2010-038.txt (a plain-text conversion of the HTML view, not the
-- authorised PDF).
--
-- NOT ENCODED: see NOTES.md (Scope). In short: chapter 5 (the oversight agency), the entities' internal duties (ss 28,
-- 29, 33), s 64, offence procedure (ss 68-70), regulations (s 71), repeal and transitional provisions (chs 7, 8).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the discloser's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Public Interest Disclosure Act 2010 (Qld) -- the discloser`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 12, 13, 19 -- can I make a public interest disclosure about this`

-- s 12(1) (any person): "(a) a substantial and specific danger to the health or safety of a person with a disability;
-- (b) the commission of an offence against a provision mentioned in schedule 2, if the commission of the offence is or
-- would be a substantial and specific danger to the environment; (c) a contravention of a condition imposed under a
-- provision mentioned in schedule 2, if the contravention is or would be a substantial and specific danger to the
-- environment; (d) the conduct of another person that could, if proved, be a reprisal."
-- s 13(1) (a public officer): "(a) the conduct of another person that could, if proved, be -- (i) corrupt conduct; or
-- (ii) maladministration that adversely affects a person's interests in a substantial and specific way; or (b) a
-- substantial misuse of public resources (other than an alleged misuse based on mere disagreement over policy that may
-- properly be adopted about amounts, purposes or priorities of expenditure); or (c) a substantial and specific danger to
-- public health or safety; or (d) a substantial and specific danger to the environment."
-- s 19(1) (an employee of a GOC or rail government entity): "(a) the conduct of another employee of the GOC or rail
-- government entity, that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct; or (b) the conduct of another person that could, if
-- proved, be a reprisal that relates to a previous disclosure made by the employee to the GOC, rail government entity or
-- the Crime and Corruption Commission."
-- "corrupt conduct" is borrowed from the Crime and Corruption Act 2001, s 15 (asked); "maladministration" is defined in
-- schedule 4 (administrative action that was unlawful, unreasonable, unjust, oppressive, improperly discriminatory, for
-- an improper purpose, unexplained, mistaken or wrong); "disability" and "environment" are borrowed (asked).
DECLARE `What my information is about` IS ONE OF
    `a danger to the health or safety of a person with a disability`
    `an offence against a provision listed in schedule 2 that endangers the environment`
    `a contravention of a condition imposed under a provision listed in schedule 2 that endangers the environment`
    `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be a reprisal`
    `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct`
    `maladministration that adversely affects someone's interests`
    `a misuse of public resources`
    `a danger to public health or safety`
    `a danger to the environment`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Can I make a public interest disclosure` IS ONE OF
    `yes, as any person, under s 12: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
    `yes, as a public officer, under s 13: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
    `yes, as an employee of a GOC or rail government entity, under s 19: you may disclose it to the GOC or rail government entity or to the Crime and Corruption Commission`
    `no: you have information about it only if you honestly believe on reasonable grounds that the information tends to show it, or the information does tend to show it (ss 12(3), 13(3), 19(3))`
    `no: the danger, misuse or effect must be substantial, and where the section says so specific (ss 12(1), 13(1))`
    `no: an alleged misuse based on mere disagreement over spending policy is excluded (s 13(1)(b))`
    `no: only a public officer may disclose this kind of matter (s 13); anyone may disclose only a danger to a person with a disability, a schedule 2 environmental offence or contravention, or a reprisal (s 12)`
    `no: this is not a matter chapter 2 lets you disclose as a public interest disclosure; another law may still protect you (s 10)`

-- The kinds s 12 lets any person disclose.
GIVEN what IS A `What my information is about`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 12 lists` what IF
        what EQUALS `a danger to the health or safety of a person with a disability`
    OR  what EQUALS `an offence against a provision listed in schedule 2 that endangers the environment`
    OR  what EQUALS `a contravention of a condition imposed under a provision listed in schedule 2 that endangers the environment`

@export Can I make a public interest disclosure under the Queensland Public Interest Disclosure Act 2010 about what I know, and under which section?
GIVEN `I am a public officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an employee, member or officer of a Queensland public sector entity (a department, the police or ambulance service, a local council, a court, a State university or TAFE, a statutory body), or a Minister, ministerial staff member or school council member
      `I work for a GOC or rail government entity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an employee of a Queensland government owned corporation (GOC) or a rail government entity
      `it is about a fellow employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for corrupt conduct reported by a GOC or rail government entity employee: whether the person whose conduct it is is another employee of the same GOC or rail government entity
      `what it is about` IS A `What my information is about` @desc What your information is about
      `it is substantial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the danger, the misuse of resources, or the effect of the maladministration on someone's interests is substantial (and, for a danger or maladministration, specific); answer yes for a reprisal or corrupt conduct
      `only a disagreement over spending policy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a misuse of public resources: whether the claim rests merely on disagreement over a policy that may properly be adopted about the amounts, purposes or priorities of spending
      `I honestly believe it on reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you honestly believe, on reasonable grounds, that your information tends to show the conduct or matter
      `the information does tend to show it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information does in fact tend to show the conduct or matter, whatever you believe
GIVETH A `Can I make a public interest disclosure`
DECIDE `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF what EQUALS `something else`
            THEN `no: this is not a matter chapter 2 lets you disclose as a public interest disclosure; another law may still protect you (s 10)`
        IF (NOT (`I honestly believe it on reasonable grounds` OR `the information does tend to show it`))
            THEN `no: you have information about it only if you honestly believe on reasonable grounds that the information tends to show it, or the information does tend to show it (ss 12(3), 13(3), 19(3))`
        IF what EQUALS `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be a reprisal`
            THEN `yes, as any person, under s 12: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
        IF `s 12 lists` what
            THEN IF `it is substantial`
                 THEN `yes, as any person, under s 12: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
                 ELSE `no: the danger, misuse or effect must be substantial, and where the section says so specific (ss 12(1), 13(1))`
        IF `I am a public officer`
            THEN `the s 13 answer`
        IF `I work for a GOC or rail government entity` AND what EQUALS `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct` AND `it is about a fellow employee`
            THEN `yes, as an employee of a GOC or rail government entity, under s 19: you may disclose it to the GOC or rail government entity or to the Crime and Corruption Commission`
        OTHERWISE `no: only a public officer may disclose this kind of matter (s 13); anyone may disclose only a danger to a person with a disability, a schedule 2 environmental offence or contravention, or a reprisal (s 12)`
    WHERE
        what MEANS `what it is about`
        `the s 13 answer` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF what EQUALS `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct`
                    THEN `yes, as a public officer, under s 13: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
                IF what EQUALS `a misuse of public resources` AND `only a disagreement over spending policy`
                    THEN `no: an alleged misuse based on mere disagreement over spending policy is excluded (s 13(1)(b))`
                IF `it is substantial`
                    THEN `yes, as a public officer, under s 13: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
                OTHERWISE `no: the danger, misuse or effect must be substantial, and where the section says so specific (ss 12(1), 13(1))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 5, 6, 14-16, 19(2), 20 -- to whom can I make it`

-- s 14: "(1) A member of the Legislative Assembly is a proper authority to whom a person may make any disclosure under
-- section 12 or 13. (2) However, subsection (1) does not apply to a disclosure if the information that is the subject of
-- the disclosure relates to a judicial officer."
-- s 15(1): "A public sector entity is a proper authority to which a person may make a disclosure under section 12 or 13
-- if -- (a) the information ... relates to -- (i) the conduct of the entity or any of its public officers; or (ii)
-- anything the entity has a power to investigate or remedy; or (iii) the conduct of another person that could, if
-- proved, be a reprisal that relates to a previous disclosure made by the person to a proper authority; or (b) the person
-- honestly believes that the information ... relates to a matter mentioned in paragraph (a)." (2) excludes judicial
-- officers.
-- s 16(2): "A disclosure relating to a judicial officer that could, if the conduct were proved, be corrupt conduct or a
-- reprisal relating to a previous disclosure under this Act, may be made only -- (a) to the chief judicial officer of the
-- relevant court or tribunal; or (b) to the Crime and Corruption Commission." (3): "Another disclosure relating to a
-- judicial officer may be made only to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal." (4): "A chief
-- judicial officer ... may receive a public interest disclosure only if the disclosure is about the conduct of another
-- judicial officer of the court or tribunal."
-- s 6(2): "the following are not public sector entities -- ... (b) a GOC, other than to the extent expressly stated in
-- this Act".
DECLARE `The route I disclose under` IS ONE OF
    `s 12 or s 13`
    `s 19 (a GOC or rail government entity employee)`

DECLARE `Who I would tell` IS ONE OF
    `a member of the Legislative Assembly (a State MP)`
    `the Crime and Corruption Commission`
    `another public sector entity (a department, council, statutory body, the Ombudsman, a court or the like)`
    `the chief judicial officer of the judicial officer's court or tribunal`
    `the GOC I work for`
    `the rail government entity I work for`
    `a journalist`

DECLARE `Is this a proper authority for my disclosure` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a member of the Legislative Assembly is a proper authority for any disclosure under s 12 or 13 (s 14(1))`
    `yes: this public sector entity is a proper authority because of its connection with the information (s 15(1))`
    `yes: a disclosure about a judicial officer that could be corrupt conduct or a reprisal may be made to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal or to the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 16(2))`
    `yes: any other disclosure about a judicial officer may be made only to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal (s 16(3))`
    `yes: under s 19 you may disclose to the GOC or rail government entity or to the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 19(2))`
    `no: a disclosure about a judicial officer may be made only to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal, or, for corrupt conduct or a reprisal, also to the Crime and Corruption Commission (ss 14(2), 15(2), 16(2), (3))`
    `no: a chief judicial officer may receive a disclosure only about another judicial officer of the court or tribunal (s 16(4))`
    `no: a public sector entity is a proper authority only if the information relates to it, its officers, something it can investigate or remedy, or a reprisal for an earlier disclosure, or you honestly believe so (s 15(1))`
    `no: a GOC is not a public sector entity (s 6(2)(b)), so it is not a proper authority for a disclosure under s 12 or 13; s 19 is the route for its employees`
    `no: s 19 names only the GOC or rail government entity and the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 19(2))`
    `not yet: a journalist may receive a disclosure only under s 20, after a disclosure to a proper authority was not investigated, ended without action, or was not answered within 6 months`
    `not covered: whether a rail government entity is a public sector entity turns on the Transport Infrastructure Act 1994, not encoded`

@export Is this person or body a proper authority to whom I can make my public interest disclosure (an MP, the Crime and Corruption Commission, a department, a chief judge, my GOC, a journalist)?
GIVEN `the route` IS A `The route I disclose under` @desc The section you may disclose under, as the question "Can I make a public interest disclosure" answered: s 12 or s 13, or s 19 for a GOC or rail government entity employee
      `the recipient` IS A `Who I would tell` @desc Whom you would make the disclosure to
      `it is about a judicial officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information relates to the conduct of a judge, magistrate, tribunal member, or a registrar doing delegated judicial tasks
      `the judicial officer is the chief judicial officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a disclosure about a judicial officer: whether that officer is the chief judicial officer of the court or tribunal (for example the Chief Justice, the Chief Judge or the Chief Magistrate)
      `it could be corrupt conduct or a reprisal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a disclosure about a judicial officer: whether the conduct, if proved, could be corrupt conduct, or a reprisal for an earlier disclosure under this Act
      `the entity is connected with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a public sector entity or the Crime and Corruption Commission: whether the information relates to the entity's own conduct or its officers', to anything it has power to investigate or remedy, or to a reprisal for an earlier disclosure you made to a proper authority; or you honestly believe so
GIVETH A `Is this a proper authority for my disclosure`
DECIDE `Is this a proper authority question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the recipient` EQUALS `a journalist`
            THEN `not yet: a journalist may receive a disclosure only under s 20, after a disclosure to a proper authority was not investigated, ended without action, or was not answered within 6 months`
        IF `the route` EQUALS `s 19 (a GOC or rail government entity employee)`
            THEN IF `the recipient` EQUALS `the GOC I work for` OR `the recipient` EQUALS `the rail government entity I work for` OR `the recipient` EQUALS `the Crime and Corruption Commission`
                 THEN `yes: under s 19 you may disclose to the GOC or rail government entity or to the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 19(2))`
                 ELSE `no: s 19 names only the GOC or rail government entity and the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 19(2))`
        IF `it is about a judicial officer`
            THEN `the s 16 answer`
        IF `the recipient` EQUALS `a member of the Legislative Assembly (a State MP)`
            THEN `yes: a member of the Legislative Assembly is a proper authority for any disclosure under s 12 or 13 (s 14(1))`
        IF `the recipient` EQUALS `the chief judicial officer of the judicial officer's court or tribunal`
            THEN `no: a chief judicial officer may receive a disclosure only about another judicial officer of the court or tribunal (s 16(4))`
        IF `the recipient` EQUALS `the GOC I work for`
            THEN `no: a GOC is not a public sector entity (s 6(2)(b)), so it is not a proper authority for a disclosure under s 12 or 13; s 19 is the route for its employees`
        IF `the recipient` EQUALS `the rail government entity I work for`
            THEN `not covered: whether a rail government entity is a public sector entity turns on the Transport Infrastructure Act 1994, not encoded`
        IF `the entity is connected with it`
            THEN `yes: this public sector entity is a proper authority because of its connection with the information (s 15(1))`
        OTHERWISE `no: a public sector entity is a proper authority only if the information relates to it, its officers, something it can investigate or remedy, or a reprisal for an earlier disclosure, or you honestly believe so (s 15(1))`
    WHERE
        `the s 16 answer` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `the recipient` EQUALS `the chief judicial officer of the judicial officer's court or tribunal` AND `the judicial officer is the chief judicial officer`
                    THEN `no: a chief judicial officer may receive a disclosure only about another judicial officer of the court or tribunal (s 16(4))`
                IF `the recipient` EQUALS `the chief judicial officer of the judicial officer's court or tribunal` AND `it could be corrupt conduct or a reprisal`
                    THEN `yes: a disclosure about a judicial officer that could be corrupt conduct or a reprisal may be made to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal or to the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 16(2))`
                IF `the recipient` EQUALS `the chief judicial officer of the judicial officer's court or tribunal`
                    THEN `yes: any other disclosure about a judicial officer may be made only to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal (s 16(3))`
                IF `the recipient` EQUALS `the Crime and Corruption Commission` AND `it could be corrupt conduct or a reprisal`
                    THEN `yes: a disclosure about a judicial officer that could be corrupt conduct or a reprisal may be made to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal or to the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 16(2))`
                OTHERWISE `no: a disclosure about a judicial officer may be made only to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal, or, for corrupt conduct or a reprisal, also to the Crime and Corruption Commission (ss 14(2), 15(2), 16(2), (3))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 17, 19(4), (5) -- how must I make it`

-- s 17: "(1) A person may make a disclosure to a proper authority in any way, including anonymously. (2) However, if a
-- proper authority has a reasonable procedure for making a public interest disclosure to the proper authority, the
-- person must use the procedure. (3) Despite subsection (2), if the proper authority is a public sector entity, the
-- person may make the disclosure to -- (a) its chief executive officer; or (b) for a public sector entity that is a
-- department -- the Minister responsible for its administration; or (c) if the proper authority that is a public sector
-- entity has a governing body -- a member of its governing body; or (d) if the person is an officer of the entity --
-- another person who, directly or indirectly, supervises or manages the person; or (e) an officer of the entity who has
-- the function of receiving or taking action on the type of information being disclosed."
-- s 19(4), (5) say the same for a disclosure to a GOC or rail government entity, without the (3) alternatives.
-- Whether a procedure is "reasonable" is asked.
DECLARE `The kind of proper authority` IS ONE OF
    `a member of the Legislative Assembly`
    `a public sector entity`
    `a GOC or rail government entity, under s 19`

DECLARE `The way I would make it` IS ONE OF
    `through the authority's own procedure for public interest disclosures`
    `to the entity's chief executive officer`
    `to the Minister responsible for the department`
    `to a member of the entity's governing body`
    `to someone who directly or indirectly supervises or manages me`
    `to an officer whose function is to receive or act on this kind of information`
    `some other way`

DECLARE `May I make it this way` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the authority has no reasonable procedure, so you may make it in any way, including anonymously (s 17(1), s 19(4))`
    `yes: through the authority's procedure (s 17(2), s 19(5))`
    `yes: despite the authority's procedure, s 17(3) lets you make it to this person in the public sector entity`
    `no: the authority has a reasonable procedure and you must use it (s 17(2), s 19(5)); this way is not one s 17(3) allows`

@export May I make my public interest disclosure this way (anonymously, to the chief executive, to my manager, to the Minister), or must I use the authority's procedure?
GIVEN `the authority` IS A `The kind of proper authority` @desc The kind of proper authority you are making the disclosure to
      `it has a reasonable procedure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authority has a reasonable procedure for making a public interest disclosure to it (for example a published whistleblower policy)
      `the way` IS A `The way I would make it` @desc How, or to whom within the authority, you would make the disclosure
      `it is a department` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a public sector entity: whether it is a Queensland government department
      `it has a governing body` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a public sector entity: whether it has a governing body, such as a board or council
      `I am one of its officers` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a public sector entity: whether you are an officer of that entity
GIVETH A `May I make it this way`
DECIDE `May I make it this way question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `it has a reasonable procedure`)
            THEN `yes: the authority has no reasonable procedure, so you may make it in any way, including anonymously (s 17(1), s 19(4))`
        IF `the way` EQUALS `through the authority's own procedure for public interest disclosures`
            THEN `yes: through the authority's procedure (s 17(2), s 19(5))`
        IF `the authority` EQUALS `a public sector entity` AND `s 17(3) allows it`
            THEN `yes: despite the authority's procedure, s 17(3) lets you make it to this person in the public sector entity`
        OTHERWISE `no: the authority has a reasonable procedure and you must use it (s 17(2), s 19(5)); this way is not one s 17(3) allows`
    WHERE
        DECIDE `s 17(3) allows it` IF
                `the way` EQUALS `to the entity's chief executive officer`
            OR  (`the way` EQUALS `to the Minister responsible for the department` AND `it is a department`)
            OR  (`the way` EQUALS `to a member of the entity's governing body` AND `it has a governing body`)
            OR  (`the way` EQUALS `to someone who directly or indirectly supervises or manages me` AND `I am one of its officers`)
            OR  `the way` EQUALS `to an officer whose function is to receive or act on this kind of information`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 30, 32 -- after I disclose: what the entity may decide and must tell me` -- arises once a disclosure is made

-- s 30(1): "A public sector entity may decide not to investigate or deal with a public interest disclosure if -- (a) the
-- substance of the disclosure has already been investigated or dealt with by another appropriate process; or (b) the
-- entity reasonably considers that the disclosure should be dealt with by another appropriate process; or (c) the age of
-- the information ... makes it impracticable to investigate; or (d) the entity reasonably considers that the disclosure
-- is too trivial to warrant investigation and that dealing with the disclosure would substantially and unreasonably
-- divert the resources of the entity ...; or (e) another entity that has jurisdiction to investigate the disclosure has
-- notified the entity that investigation of the disclosure is not warranted."
-- (2): "the entity must give written reasons for its decision to the person making the disclosure." (3): "A person who
-- receives written reasons ... may apply to the chief executive of the entity for a review of the decision within 28
-- days after receiving the written reasons."
DECLARE `May the entity decline to investigate` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the entity may decide not to investigate or deal with it, but must give you written reasons, and you may apply to its chief executive for a review within 28 days after receiving them (s 30)`
    `no: none of the s 30(1) grounds for deciding not to investigate or deal with it is met`

@export May the entity I disclosed to decide not to investigate or deal with my public interest disclosure?
GIVEN `already dealt with by another process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the substance of your disclosure has already been investigated or dealt with by another appropriate process
      `the entity considers another process should deal with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity reasonably considers that your disclosure should be dealt with by another appropriate process
      `too old to investigate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the age of the information makes it impracticable to investigate
      `the entity considers it too trivial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity reasonably considers your disclosure too trivial to warrant investigation AND that dealing with it would substantially and unreasonably divert its resources from its functions
      `another entity said investigation is not warranted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another entity with jurisdiction to investigate it has notified the entity that investigation is not warranted
GIVETH A `May the entity decline to investigate`
DECIDE `May the entity decline to investigate question` IS
    IF     `already dealt with by another process`
        OR `the entity considers another process should deal with it`
        OR `too old to investigate`
        OR `the entity considers it too trivial`
        OR `another entity said investigation is not warranted`
    THEN `yes: the entity may decide not to investigate or deal with it, but must give you written reasons, and you may apply to its chief executive for a review within 28 days after receiving them (s 30)`
    ELSE `no: none of the s 30(1) grounds for deciding not to investigate or deal with it is met`

-- s 30(3): "within 28 days after receiving the written reasons".
GIVEN received IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 30 review deadline for reasons received on` received MEANS `the nth day after` 28 received

@export By what date must I apply to the chief executive for a review of the decision not to investigate or deal with my disclosure?
GIVEN `the day I received the written reasons` IS A DATE @desc The date you received the entity's written reasons for deciding not to investigate or deal with your disclosure (ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-03-02)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must I apply for a review question` IS `the s 30 review deadline for reasons received on` `the day I received the written reasons`

@export Is my application for a review of the decision not to investigate my disclosure in time?
GIVEN `the day I received the written reasons` IS A DATE @desc The date you received the entity's written reasons (ISO 8601)
      `the day I applied` IS A DATE @desc The date you applied, or will apply, to the entity's chief executive for a review (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my review application in time question` IF
        `the day I applied` `falls on or before` (`the s 30 review deadline for reasons received on` `the day I received the written reasons`)
    AND `the day I received the written reasons` `falls on or before` `the day I applied`

-- s 32: "(1) A public sector entity to which a public interest disclosure is made under section 15, or referred under
-- section 31 or 34, must give the person who made the disclosure, or the entity that referred the disclosure, reasonable
-- information about the disclosure. (2) ... includes at least -- (a) confirmation that the disclosure was received ...;
-- (b) a description of the action proposed to be taken, or taken ...; (c) if action has been taken ... -- a description
-- of the results of the action. (3) The information must be given ... in writing. (4) The public sector entity need not
-- give information under subsection (1) if giving the information would be likely to adversely affect -- (a) anybody's
-- safety; or (b) the investigation of an offence or possible offence; or (c) necessary confidentiality about an
-- informant's existence or identity. (5) If a person makes a public interest disclosure to the Crime and Corruption
-- Commission in a complaint of corruption, this section does not impose on the commission any duty that the commission
-- does not already have under the Crime and Corruption Act 2001."
DECLARE `How my disclosure reached the entity` IS ONE OF
    `I made it to the public sector entity myself (s 15)`
    `another entity or a member of Parliament referred it to the entity (s 31 or s 34)`
    `I made it to the Crime and Corruption Commission in a complaint of corruption`
    `I made it to a member of the Legislative Assembly`
    `I made it to a GOC or rail government entity under s 19`

DECLARE `What must I be told` IS ONE OF
    `yes: in writing, at least confirmation that it was received and a description of the action proposed or taken (s 32(1)-(3))`
    `yes: in writing, at least confirmation that it was received, a description of the action taken, and its results (s 32(1)-(3))`
    `either you or the entity that referred it: s 32(1) requires the information to be given to the person who made the disclosure, or the entity that referred it, and does not say which`
    `no: the entity need not tell you, because giving the information would be likely to adversely affect someone's safety, the investigation of an offence, or necessary confidentiality about an informant (s 32(4))`
    `no: a member of the Legislative Assembly has no duty under the Act to tell you anything; the member may refer it to an entity, which then has the s 32 duty (ss 32, 34)`
    `no: s 32 applies to a disclosure made under s 15 or referred under s 31 or 34, not to one made to a GOC or rail government entity under s 19`
    `not covered: s 32 adds no duty the Crime and Corruption Commission does not already have under the Crime and Corruption Act 2001, not encoded (s 32(5))`

@export Must the entity that received my public interest disclosure tell me what it is doing about it, and what must it tell me?
GIVEN `how it reached them` IS A `How my disclosure reached the entity` @desc How your disclosure came to the entity you are asking about
      `it has taken action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity has already taken action on your disclosure
      `telling me would be likely to harm safety, an investigation or an informant's confidentiality` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether giving you the information would be likely to adversely affect anybody's safety, the investigation of an offence or possible offence, or necessary confidentiality about an informant's existence or identity
GIVETH A `What must I be told`
DECIDE `What must I be told question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `how it reached them` EQUALS `I made it to the Crime and Corruption Commission in a complaint of corruption`
            THEN `not covered: s 32 adds no duty the Crime and Corruption Commission does not already have under the Crime and Corruption Act 2001, not encoded (s 32(5))`
        IF `how it reached them` EQUALS `I made it to a member of the Legislative Assembly`
            THEN `no: a member of the Legislative Assembly has no duty under the Act to tell you anything; the member may refer it to an entity, which then has the s 32 duty (ss 32, 34)`
        IF `how it reached them` EQUALS `I made it to a GOC or rail government entity under s 19`
            THEN `no: s 32 applies to a disclosure made under s 15 or referred under s 31 or 34, not to one made to a GOC or rail government entity under s 19`
        IF `telling me would be likely to harm safety, an investigation or an informant's confidentiality`
            THEN `no: the entity need not tell you, because giving the information would be likely to adversely affect someone's safety, the investigation of an offence, or necessary confidentiality about an informant (s 32(4))`
        IF `how it reached them` EQUALS `another entity or a member of Parliament referred it to the entity (s 31 or s 34)`
            THEN `either you or the entity that referred it: s 32(1) requires the information to be given to the person who made the disclosure, or the entity that referred it, and does not say which`
        IF `it has taken action`
            THEN `yes: in writing, at least confirmation that it was received, a description of the action taken, and its results (s 32(1)-(3))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: in writing, at least confirmation that it was received and a description of the action proposed or taken (s 32(1)-(3))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `s 20 -- can I go to a journalist` -- arises once a disclosure is made

-- s 20(1): "This section applies if -- (a) a person has made a public interest disclosure under this chapter; and (b)
-- the entity to which the disclosure was made or, if the disclosure was referred under section 31 or 34, the entity to
-- which the disclosure was referred -- (i) decided not to investigate or deal with the disclosure; or (ii) investigated
-- the disclosure but did not recommend the taking of any action in relation to the disclosure; or (iii) did not notify
-- the person, within 6 months after the date the disclosure was made, whether or not the disclosure was to be
-- investigated or dealt with." (2): "The person may make a disclosure of substantially the same information that was the
-- subject of the public interest disclosure mentioned in subsection (1)(a) to a journalist." (3)(a): the disclosure to
-- the journalist "is a public interest disclosure".
DECLARE `What the entity did with my disclosure` IS ONE OF
    `decided not to investigate or deal with it`
    `investigated it but did not recommend any action`
    `investigated it and recommended action`
    `is investigating or dealing with it`
    `nothing I know of`

DECLARE `Can I go to a journalist` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the entity decided not to investigate or deal with your disclosure (s 20(1)(b)(i)); the disclosure to the journalist is itself a public interest disclosure (s 20(3))`
    `yes: the entity investigated your disclosure but did not recommend any action (s 20(1)(b)(ii)); the disclosure to the journalist is itself a public interest disclosure (s 20(3))`
    `yes: you were not told within 6 months after the date of your disclosure whether it would be investigated or dealt with (s 20(1)(b)(iii)); the disclosure to the journalist is itself a public interest disclosure (s 20(3))`
    `no: s 20 applies only after you have made a public interest disclosure under chapter 2 (s 20(1)(a))`
    `no: you may give a journalist only substantially the same information as your public interest disclosure (s 20(2))`
    `no: none of the s 20(1)(b) events has happened; if you are not told whether it will be investigated or dealt with, you may once the 6 months end`

-- s 20(1)(b)(iii): "within 6 months after the date the disclosure was made".
GIVEN made IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 20 notification deadline for a disclosure made on` made MEANS `six months after` made

@export By what date must the entity tell me whether my public interest disclosure will be investigated or dealt with, before I may take it to a journalist?
GIVEN `the day I made the disclosure` IS A DATE @desc The date you made your public interest disclosure (ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-01-15)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must I be told whether it will be investigated question` IS `the s 20 notification deadline for a disclosure made on` `the day I made the disclosure`

@export Can I now take my public interest disclosure to a journalist?
GIVEN `I made a public interest disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already made a public interest disclosure to a proper authority under the Act
      `the same information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what you would tell the journalist is substantially the same information as your public interest disclosure
      `what the entity did` IS A `What the entity did with my disclosure` @desc What the entity you disclosed to (or the entity it was referred to) has done with your disclosure
      `the day I made the disclosure` IS A DATE @desc The date you made your public interest disclosure (ISO 8601)
      `I have been told whether it will be investigated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity has told you whether or not your disclosure will be investigated or dealt with
      `the day I was told` IS A DATE @desc Only if you have been told: the date you were told whether it would be investigated or dealt with (ISO 8601); otherwise give today's date
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Can I go to a journalist`
DECIDE `Can I go to a journalist question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `I made a public interest disclosure`)
            THEN `no: s 20 applies only after you have made a public interest disclosure under chapter 2 (s 20(1)(a))`
        IF (NOT `the same information`)
            THEN `no: you may give a journalist only substantially the same information as your public interest disclosure (s 20(2))`
        IF `what the entity did` EQUALS `decided not to investigate or deal with it`
            THEN `yes: the entity decided not to investigate or deal with your disclosure (s 20(1)(b)(i)); the disclosure to the journalist is itself a public interest disclosure (s 20(3))`
        IF `what the entity did` EQUALS `investigated it but did not recommend any action`
            THEN `yes: the entity investigated your disclosure but did not recommend any action (s 20(1)(b)(ii)); the disclosure to the journalist is itself a public interest disclosure (s 20(3))`
        IF `not told in time`
            THEN `yes: you were not told within 6 months after the date of your disclosure whether it would be investigated or dealt with (s 20(1)(b)(iii)); the disclosure to the journalist is itself a public interest disclosure (s 20(3))`
        OTHERWISE `no: none of the s 20(1)(b) events has happened; if you are not told whether it will be investigated or dealt with, you may once the 6 months end`
    WHERE
        deadline MEANS `the s 20 notification deadline for a disclosure made on` `the day I made the disclosure`
        DECIDE `not told in time` IF
            IF `I have been told whether it will be investigated`
            THEN NOT (`the day I was told` `falls on or before` deadline)
            ELSE NOT (`today` `falls on or before` deadline)

-- The s 20 six months as a regulative rule. s 20 puts no duty on the entity to notify within 6 months (s 32 requires
-- reasonable information but sets no time), so the entity's notice is written with DO, not MUST: doing it in time closes
-- this route to a journalist (HENCE FULFILLED); not doing it in time opens it (LEST: the discloser MAY then disclose
-- substantially the same information to a journalist, with no time limit). Routes (i) and (ii) are not in this rule.
DECLARE `PID party` IS ONE OF
    `the entity that received or was referred the disclosure`
    `the discloser`

DECLARE `PID action` IS ONE OF
    `notify the discloser whether the disclosure is to be investigated or dealt with`
    `disclose substantially the same information to a journalist`

GIVEN made IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `PID party` `PID action`
`the s 20 six-month rule for a disclosure made on` made MEANS
    PARTY `the entity that received or was referred the disclosure`
    DO    `notify the discloser whether the disclosure is to be investigated or dealt with`
    WITHIN `days from` made `to` (`the s 20 notification deadline for a disclosure made on` made)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST
        PARTY `the discloser`
        MAY   `disclose substantially the same information to a journalist`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 36-39, 66 -- am I protected, and what if I get it wrong`

-- s 36: "A person who makes a public interest disclosure is not subject to any civil or criminal liability or any
-- liability arising by way of administrative process, including disciplinary action, for making the disclosure."
-- s 37: the person does not "(a) commit an offence under any Act that imposes a duty to maintain confidentiality ...; or
-- (b) breach an obligation by way of oath or rule of law or practice or under an agreement requiring the person to
-- maintain confidentiality".
-- s 38: "in a proceeding for defamation, a person who makes a public interest disclosure has a defence of absolute
-- privilege for publishing the information disclosed."
-- s 39(1): "A person's liability for the person's own conduct is not affected by the person's disclosure of that conduct
-- under this Act."
DECLARE `The risk I face` IS ONE OF
    `being sued, prosecuted or disciplined for making the disclosure`
    `breaching a confidentiality law, oath, rule or agreement by making the disclosure`
    `being sued for defamation over what I disclosed`
    `being held liable for my own wrongdoing that I disclosed`

DECLARE `Am I protected` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you are not subject to any civil, criminal or administrative liability, including disciplinary action, for making it (s 36)`
    `yes: you do not commit a confidentiality offence, or breach a confidentiality oath, rule or agreement, by making it (s 37)`
    `yes: you have a defence of absolute privilege for publishing the information disclosed (s 38)`
    `no: your liability for your own conduct is not affected by your disclosing it (s 39)`
    `not covered: the Act's protections attach to a public interest disclosure; another law may protect a disclosure that is not one (s 10)`

@export Am I protected from being sued, prosecuted, disciplined, or sued for defamation, for making my public interest disclosure?
GIVEN `it is a public interest disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your disclosure is a public interest disclosure under the Act (see the questions on what you may disclose and to whom)
      `the risk` IS A `The risk I face` @desc The liability you are worried about
GIVETH A `Am I protected`
DECIDE `Am I protected question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `it is a public interest disclosure`)
            THEN `not covered: the Act's protections attach to a public interest disclosure; another law may protect a disclosure that is not one (s 10)`
        IF `the risk` EQUALS `being sued, prosecuted or disciplined for making the disclosure`
            THEN `yes: you are not subject to any civil, criminal or administrative liability, including disciplinary action, for making it (s 36)`
        IF `the risk` EQUALS `breaching a confidentiality law, oath, rule or agreement by making the disclosure`
            THEN `yes: you do not commit a confidentiality offence, or breach a confidentiality oath, rule or agreement, by making it (s 37)`
        IF `the risk` EQUALS `being sued for defamation over what I disclosed`
            THEN `yes: you have a defence of absolute privilege for publishing the information disclosed (s 38)`
        OTHERWISE `no: your liability for your own conduct is not affected by your disclosing it (s 39)`

-- s 66(1): "A person must not -- (a) make a statement to a proper authority intending that it be acted on as a public
-- interest disclosure; and (b) in the statement, or in the course of inquiries into the statement, intentionally give
-- information that is false or misleading in a material particular. Maximum penalty -- 167 penalty units or 2 years
-- imprisonment." (2): indictable. s 69(1): heard and decided summarily, unless the Magistrates Court abstains (s 69(2)).
-- s 67(1): a public officer who contravenes s 66 "is guilty of misconduct". The value of a penalty unit is set by the
-- Penalties and Sentences Act 1992 and its regulation, not deposited; the answer gives units.
DECLARE `Could I commit the false information offence` IS ONE OF
    `yes: an indictable offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment, ordinarily heard and decided summarily (ss 66, 69)`
    `yes: an indictable offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment, ordinarily heard and decided summarily (ss 66, 69); as a public officer it is also misconduct for which you may be disciplined or dismissed (s 67)`
    `no: s 66 is committed only by intentionally giving information that is false or misleading in a material particular, in a statement made to a proper authority to be acted on as a public interest disclosure, or in inquiries into it`

@export Could I be prosecuted for giving false or misleading information in my public interest disclosure, and what is the maximum penalty?
GIVEN `I meant it to be acted on as a public interest disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made to a proper authority intending that it be acted on as a public interest disclosure
      `the information was false or misleading in a material particular` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether information you gave in the statement, or in inquiries into it, was false or misleading in a way that matters
      `I gave it intentionally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave that false or misleading information intentionally (not by honest mistake)
      `I am a public officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a public officer (an employee, member or officer of a Queensland public sector entity)
GIVETH A `Could I commit the false information offence`
DECIDE `Could I commit the false information offence question` IS
    IF      `I meant it to be acted on as a public interest disclosure`
        AND `the information was false or misleading in a material particular`
        AND `I gave it intentionally`
    THEN IF `I am a public officer`
         THEN `yes: an indictable offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment, ordinarily heard and decided summarily (ss 66, 69); as a public officer it is also misconduct for which you may be disciplined or dismissed (s 67)`
         ELSE `yes: an indictable offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment, ordinarily heard and decided summarily (ss 66, 69)`
    ELSE `no: s 66 is committed only by intentionally giving information that is false or misleading in a material particular, in a statement made to a proper authority to be acted on as a public interest disclosure, or in inquiries into it`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 40, 41, 45 -- was what was done to me a reprisal` -- arises if something has been done or threatened

-- s 40(1): "A person must not cause, or attempt or conspire to cause, detriment to another person because, or in the
-- belief that -- (a) the other person or someone else has made, or intends to make, a public interest disclosure; or (b)
-- the other person or someone else is, has been, or intends to be, involved in a proceeding under the Act against any
-- person." (2): "An attempt to cause detriment includes an attempt to induce a person to cause detriment." (3): "A
-- contravention of subsection (1) is a reprisal". (5): "it is sufficient if the unlawful ground is a substantial ground
-- for the act or omission that is the reprisal, even if there is another ground for the act or omission."
-- Schedule 4: "detriment includes -- (a) personal injury or prejudice to safety; and (b) property damage or loss; and (c)
-- intimidation or harassment; and (d) adverse discrimination, disadvantage or adverse treatment about career,
-- profession, employment, trade or business; and (e) financial loss; and (f) damage to reputation".
-- s 41(1): "A person must not take a reprisal. Maximum penalty -- 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment."
-- s 45(2): "a manager may take reasonable management action in relation to an employee who has made a public interest
-- disclosure only if the manager's reasons for taking the action do not include the fact that the person has made the
-- public interest disclosure."
DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `personal injury or prejudice to my safety`
    `damage to or loss of my property`
    `intimidation or harassment`
    `adverse discrimination, disadvantage or adverse treatment about my career, profession, employment, trade or business`
    `financial loss`
    `damage to my reputation`
    `something else that disadvantaged me`

DECLARE `How far it went` IS ONE OF
    `it was done to me`
    `someone attempted or conspired to do it, or tried to get someone else to do it`

-- The "unlawful ground" (s 40(4)): the disclosure (made or intended, by you or anyone), or involvement in a proceeding
-- under the Act, or the belief in either.
DECLARE `The part the disclosure played` IS ONE OF
    `it was the only or main reason`
    `it was a substantial reason, among others`
    `it was only a minor reason`
    `it played no part`

DECLARE `Was it a reprisal` IS ONE OF
    `yes: causing you detriment on that ground is a reprisal (s 40); it is an offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment (s 41), and a tort (s 42)`
    `yes: attempting or conspiring to cause you detriment, including trying to induce someone else to, is a reprisal (s 40(1), (2)); it is an offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment (s 41)`
    `no: your manager may take reasonable management action where the manager's reasons do not include your disclosure (s 45)`
    `no: it was not done because, or in the belief that, a public interest disclosure was or would be made, or someone was or would be involved in a proceeding under the Act (s 40(1))`
    `not covered: s 40(5) makes a substantial ground enough; the Act does not say whether a ground that was only minor is`
    `not covered: detriment is defined only by an inclusive list (schedule 4); whether this is detriment is for a court`

@export Was what was done to me (or attempted against me) a reprisal for a public interest disclosure under the Queensland Public Interest Disclosure Act 2010?
GIVEN `what was done` IS A `What was done to me` @desc The harm done or attempted
      `how far it went` IS A `How far it went` @desc Whether the harm was actually done to you, or only attempted, conspired, or someone tried to induce another person to do it
      `the part the disclosure played` IS A `The part the disclosure played` @desc How far the reason for it was that you or someone else made or intended to make a public interest disclosure, or was or would be involved in a proceeding under the Act (or the belief that this was so)
      `it was reasonable management action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was reasonable management action by your manager (for example a reasonable performance appraisal, counselling requirement, suspension, disciplinary action, transfer or redundancy)
GIVETH A `Was it a reprisal`
DECIDE `Was it a reprisal question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the part the disclosure played` EQUALS `it played no part` AND `it was reasonable management action`
            THEN `no: your manager may take reasonable management action where the manager's reasons do not include your disclosure (s 45)`
        IF `the part the disclosure played` EQUALS `it played no part`
            THEN `no: it was not done because, or in the belief that, a public interest disclosure was or would be made, or someone was or would be involved in a proceeding under the Act (s 40(1))`
        IF `what was done` EQUALS `something else that disadvantaged me`
            THEN `not covered: detriment is defined only by an inclusive list (schedule 4); whether this is detriment is for a court`
        IF `the part the disclosure played` EQUALS `it was only a minor reason`
            THEN `not covered: s 40(5) makes a substantial ground enough; the Act does not say whether a ground that was only minor is`
        IF `how far it went` EQUALS `it was done to me`
            THEN `yes: causing you detriment on that ground is a reprisal (s 40); it is an offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment (s 41), and a tort (s 42)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: attempting or conspiring to cause you detriment, including trying to induce someone else to, is a reprisal (s 40(1), (2)); it is an offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment (s 41)`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 42-44 -- can I claim damages, and from whom` -- arises if a reprisal has been taken

-- s 42: "(1) A reprisal is a tort and a person who takes a reprisal is liable in damages to any person who suffers
-- detriment as a result. (2) Any appropriate remedy that may be granted by a court for a tort, including exemplary
-- damages, may be granted ... (3) If the claim for damages goes to trial in the Supreme Court or the District Court, it
-- must be decided by a judge sitting without a jury. ... (5) Proceedings for damages may be brought under this section
-- even if a prosecution ... has not been brought, or can not be brought ... (6) The Workers' Compensation and
-- Rehabilitation Act 2003 does not apply to proceedings for damages brought under this section."
-- s 44(3)(b): "if the person makes a complaint under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about a reprisal and the complaint
-- is accepted under that Act, the person can not subsequently commence proceedings under section 42".
-- No limitation period is in this Act; the Limitation of Actions Act 1974 governs (not encoded here).
DECLARE `Can I claim damages` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a reprisal is a tort; you may claim damages, including exemplary damages, and any remedy a court may grant for a tort; a trial in the Supreme or District Court is by judge alone; no prosecution is needed; the Workers' Compensation and Rehabilitation Act 2003 does not apply (s 42)`
    `no: your complaint under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal was accepted, so you can not now commence proceedings under s 42 (s 44(3)(b))`
    `no: damages under s 42 go to a person who suffers detriment as a result of a reprisal; an attempt that caused you no detriment gives none`
    `no: on these facts what was done was not a reprisal (s 40)`
    `not covered: whether what was done was a reprisal is open on these facts (see the reprisal question)`

@export Can I sue for damages for a reprisal taken against me for a public interest disclosure?
GIVEN `what was done` IS A `What was done to me` @desc The harm done or attempted
      `how far it went` IS A `How far it went` @desc Whether the harm was actually done to you, or only attempted, conspired, or someone tried to induce another person to do it
      `the part the disclosure played` IS A `The part the disclosure played` @desc How far the reason for it was that you or someone else made or intended to make a public interest disclosure, or was or would be involved in a proceeding under the Act
      `it was reasonable management action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was reasonable management action by your manager
      `I suffered detriment as a result` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have in fact suffered harm (injury, loss, harassment, career damage, financial loss, damage to reputation) as a result
      `my anti-discrimination complaint was accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made a complaint about the reprisal under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 and it was accepted under that Act
GIVETH A `Can I claim damages`
DECIDE `Can I claim damages question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the reprisal answer` EQUALS `no: your manager may take reasonable management action where the manager's reasons do not include your disclosure (s 45)`
            OR `the reprisal answer` EQUALS `no: it was not done because, or in the belief that, a public interest disclosure was or would be made, or someone was or would be involved in a proceeding under the Act (s 40(1))`
            THEN `no: on these facts what was done was not a reprisal (s 40)`
        IF `the reprisal answer` EQUALS `not covered: s 40(5) makes a substantial ground enough; the Act does not say whether a ground that was only minor is`
            OR `the reprisal answer` EQUALS `not covered: detriment is defined only by an inclusive list (schedule 4); whether this is detriment is for a court`
            THEN `not covered: whether what was done was a reprisal is open on these facts (see the reprisal question)`
        IF `my anti-discrimination complaint was accepted`
            THEN `no: your complaint under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal was accepted, so you can not now commence proceedings under s 42 (s 44(3)(b))`
        IF (NOT `I suffered detriment as a result`)
            THEN `no: damages under s 42 go to a person who suffers detriment as a result of a reprisal; an attempt that caused you no detriment gives none`
        OTHERWISE `yes: a reprisal is a tort; you may claim damages, including exemplary damages, and any remedy a court may grant for a tort; a trial in the Supreme or District Court is by judge alone; no prosecution is needed; the Workers' Compensation and Rehabilitation Act 2003 does not apply (s 42)`
    WHERE
        `the reprisal answer` MEANS `Was it a reprisal question` `what was done` `how far it went` `the part the disclosure played` `it was reasonable management action`

-- s 43: "(1) If any of a public sector entity's employees contravenes section 40 in the course of employment, both the
-- public sector entity and the employee ... are jointly and severally civilly liable for the contravention, and a
-- proceeding under section 42 may be taken against either or both. (2) It is a defence to a proceeding against a public
-- sector entity under section 42 if the public sector entity proves, on the balance of probabilities, that the public
-- sector entity took reasonable steps to prevent the employee contravening section 40. (3) In this section -- public
-- sector entity includes a GOC."
DECLARE `Whom can I sue` IS ONE OF
    `the person who took the reprisal (s 42(1))`
    `the person who took the reprisal and the public sector entity or GOC that employs them, jointly and severally: you may sue either or both (s 43(1))`
    `the person who took the reprisal; the employing public sector entity or GOC is liable too, but has a defence because it proves it took reasonable steps to prevent the reprisal (s 43(2))`

@export Whom can I sue for a reprisal: the person who took it, their public sector employer, or both?
GIVEN `they are an employee of a public sector entity or GOC` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who took the reprisal is an employee of a Queensland public sector entity or government owned corporation (GOC)
      `they acted in the course of employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they took the reprisal in the course of that employment
      `the employer proves it took reasonable steps to prevent it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer proves, on the balance of probabilities, that it took reasonable steps to prevent the reprisal (a court decides this)
GIVETH A `Whom can I sue`
DECIDE `Whom can I sue question` IS
    IF `they are an employee of a public sector entity or GOC` AND `they acted in the course of employment`
    THEN IF `the employer proves it took reasonable steps to prevent it`
         THEN `the person who took the reprisal; the employing public sector entity or GOC is liable too, but has a defence because it proves it took reasonable steps to prevent the reprisal (s 43(2))`
         ELSE `the person who took the reprisal and the public sector entity or GOC that employs them, jointly and severally: you may sue either or both (s 43(1))`
    ELSE `the person who took the reprisal (s 42(1))`

-- s 44: "(1) A person may make a complaint under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about a reprisal. ... (3) However --
-- (a) if a person commences proceedings in a court under section 42 in relation to a reprisal, the person can not
-- subsequently make a complaint under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal; and (b) [above]. (4) A
-- complaint ... may be made even if a prosecution ... has not been brought, or can not be brought". s 50: "a person may
-- not apply for an injunction about a reprisal under either of those provisions if the person makes a complaint under
-- the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal."
DECLARE `Can I complain under the Anti-Discrimination Act` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may complain under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal, even without a prosecution (s 44(1), (4)); but once you complain you may not apply for an injunction (s 50), and if the complaint is accepted you can not later sue under s 42 (s 44(3)(b))`
    `no: having commenced proceedings in a court under s 42 about the reprisal, you can not now complain about it under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (s 44(3)(a))`

@export Can I make a complaint about a reprisal under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 instead of suing, and what do I give up?
GIVEN `I have started court proceedings under s 42` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already commenced court proceedings for damages for the reprisal under s 42 of the Public Interest Disclosure Act 2010
GIVETH A `Can I complain under the Anti-Discrimination Act`
DECIDE `Can I complain under the Anti-Discrimination Act question` IS
    IF `I have started court proceedings under s 42`
    THEN `no: having commenced proceedings in a court under s 42 about the reprisal, you can not now complain about it under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (s 44(3)(a))`
    ELSE `yes: you may complain under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal, even without a prosecution (s 44(1), (4)); but once you complain you may not apply for an injunction (s 50), and if the complaint is accepted you can not later sue under s 42 (s 44(3)(b))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 46-50 -- appeals, relocation and injunctions` -- arises if a reprisal has been taken or is feared

-- s 46: "(1) This section applies to a public officer who, under an Act, may appeal against, or apply for a review of,
-- any of the following actions -- (a) disciplinary action taken against the public officer; (b) the appointment or
-- transfer of the public officer or another public officer to a position as a public officer; (c) unfair treatment of
-- the public officer. (2) Whether or not the Act specifies grounds for the appeal or review, the public officer may also
-- appeal the action or apply to have the action set aside because it was the taking of a reprisal against the public
-- officer. (3) Subsection (2) applies even if the decision on the hearing of the appeal or review is in the form of a
-- recommendation. (4) In this section -- public officer includes an employee of a GOC."
DECLARE `The action I want to challenge` IS ONE OF
    `disciplinary action against me`
    `the appointment or transfer of me or another public officer to a position`
    `unfair treatment of me`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Can I appeal on the ground it was a reprisal` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may also appeal it, or apply to have it set aside, on the ground that it was a reprisal, whatever grounds the other Act allows, and even if the decision is only a recommendation (s 46(2), (3))`
    `no: s 46 gives no appeal on these facts; it adds the reprisal ground only to a right another Act already gives a public officer or GOC employee to appeal or seek review of disciplinary action, an appointment or transfer, or unfair treatment`

@export Can I appeal a disciplinary action, transfer or unfair treatment on the ground that it was a reprisal for my public interest disclosure?
GIVEN `I am a public officer or GOC employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a public officer of a Queensland public sector entity, or an employee of a government owned corporation (GOC)
      `the action` IS A `The action I want to challenge` @desc The action you want to challenge
      `another Act lets me appeal or seek review of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act (for example the Public Sector Act 2022 or the Industrial Relations Act 2016) already gives you a right to appeal against, or apply for a review of, that action
GIVETH A `Can I appeal on the ground it was a reprisal`
DECIDE `Can I appeal on the ground it was a reprisal question` IS
    IF      `I am a public officer or GOC employee`
        AND (NOT (`the action` EQUALS `something else`))
        AND `another Act lets me appeal or seek review of it`
    THEN `yes: you may also appeal it, or apply to have it set aside, on the ground that it was a reprisal, whatever grounds the other Act allows, and even if the decision is only a recommendation (s 46(2), (3))`
    ELSE `no: s 46 gives no appeal on these facts; it adds the reprisal ground only to a right another Act already gives a public officer or GOC employee to appeal or seek review of disciplinary action, an appointment or transfer, or unfair treatment`

-- s 47: "(1) This section gives a public service employee a right to apply for relocation. (2) The application must be
-- made on the ground that -- (a) it is likely a reprisal will be taken against the public service employee if the
-- employee continues in the employee's existing work location; and (b) the only practical way to remove or
-- substantially remove the danger of a reprisal is to relocate the employee. (3) The application may be made ... to the
-- industrial relations commission ... (5) If the industrial relations commission considers the ground is established,
-- the commission may direct that the employee be relocated within the employee's department or to another department.
-- (6) The industrial relations commission can not direct that the employee be relocated without the agreement of -- (a)
-- the public service employee; and (b) if the relocation is to another department -- the other department's chief
-- executive." The employee's own agreement is taken as given by the application.
DECLARE `Can I be relocated` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the industrial relations commission may direct that you be relocated within your department (s 47(5))`
    `yes: the industrial relations commission may direct that you be relocated to the other department, whose chief executive agrees (s 47(5), (6))`
    `no: the commission can not direct that you be relocated to another department without that department's chief executive's agreement (s 47(6))`
    `no: the commission may direct relocation only if it considers the ground established (s 47(5))`
    `no: the application must be made on the ground that a reprisal is likely if you stay where you work and relocation is the only practical way to remove or substantially remove that danger (s 47(2))`
    `not covered: s 47 gives the right to apply for relocation to a public service employee; other employees' rights are not encoded`

@export Can I apply to the industrial relations commission to be relocated to protect me from a reprisal, and can it move me?
GIVEN `I am a public service employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a Queensland public service employee (employed in a department under the Public Sector Act 2022)
      `a reprisal is likely if I stay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is likely a reprisal will be taken against you if you stay in your existing work location
      `relocation is the only practical way` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether relocating you is the only practical way to remove, or substantially remove, the danger of a reprisal
      `the commission considers the ground established` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the industrial relations commission considers both parts of that ground established (the commission decides this)
      `to another department` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the relocation sought is to another department rather than within your own
      `that department's chief executive agrees` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a relocation to another department: whether its chief executive agrees
GIVETH A `Can I be relocated`
DECIDE `Can I be relocated question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `I am a public service employee`)
            THEN `not covered: s 47 gives the right to apply for relocation to a public service employee; other employees' rights are not encoded`
        IF (NOT (`a reprisal is likely if I stay` AND `relocation is the only practical way`))
            THEN `no: the application must be made on the ground that a reprisal is likely if you stay where you work and relocation is the only practical way to remove or substantially remove that danger (s 47(2))`
        IF (NOT `the commission considers the ground established`)
            THEN `no: the commission may direct relocation only if it considers the ground established (s 47(5))`
        IF `to another department` AND `that department's chief executive agrees`
            THEN `yes: the industrial relations commission may direct that you be relocated to the other department, whose chief executive agrees (s 47(5), (6))`
        IF `to another department`
            THEN `no: the commission can not direct that you be relocated to another department without that department's chief executive's agreement (s 47(6))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the industrial relations commission may direct that you be relocated within your department (s 47(5))`

-- s 48(1): "An application for an injunction about a reprisal may be made to the industrial commission if the reprisal
-- -- (a) has caused or may cause detriment to an employee; and (b) involves or may involve a breach of the Industrial
-- Relations Act 2016 or an industrial instrument under that Act." (2): by the employee, an industrial organisation
-- entitled to represent the employee acting with consent, or the Crime and Corruption Commission with consent where the
-- employee is a public officer and the Commission may investigate. (4): the jurisdiction "is exclusive of the
-- jurisdiction of any other court or tribunal other than the Industrial Court".
-- s 49: "(1) This section applies only to a person who can not apply under section 48 ... (2) An application for an
-- injunction about a reprisal may be made to the Supreme Court by -- (a) a person claiming that the person is suffering
-- or may suffer detriment from a reprisal; or (b) the Crime and Corruption Commission ... with the person's consent if
-- -- (i) the person is a public officer; and (ii) the reprisal involves or may involve an act or omission that the
-- Crime and Corruption Commission may investigate." s 50: no injunction after an Anti-Discrimination Act complaint.
-- s 51: the commission or court "may grant an injunction ... if it is satisfied" of a reprisal, attempt, aiding,
-- inducing or knowing involvement; s 54 an interim injunction. Whether it is granted is the court's (not decided).
DECLARE `Where can I apply for an injunction` IS ONE OF
    `the industrial commission (s 48); a union entitled to represent you may apply with your consent, and so may the Crime and Corruption Commission if you are a public officer and it may investigate the reprisal; no other court or tribunal but the Industrial Court has jurisdiction (s 48(2), (4))`
    `the Supreme Court, because you can not apply to the industrial commission (s 49); the Crime and Corruption Commission may apply with your consent if you are a public officer and it may investigate the reprisal (s 49(2)(b))`
    `nowhere under this Act: having made a complaint under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal, you may not apply for an injunction under s 48 or s 49 (s 50)`

@export Where can I apply for an injunction to stop or remedy a reprisal: the industrial commission or the Supreme Court?
GIVEN `I am an employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an employee (including under a contract of service) of the employer concerned
      `it involves a breach of industrial law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the reprisal involves, or may involve, a breach of the Industrial Relations Act 2016 or an award, agreement or other industrial instrument under it
      `I have complained under the Anti-Discrimination Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have made a complaint about the reprisal under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991
GIVETH A `Where can I apply for an injunction`
DECIDE `Where can I apply for an injunction question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `I have complained under the Anti-Discrimination Act`
            THEN `nowhere under this Act: having made a complaint under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal, you may not apply for an injunction under s 48 or s 49 (s 50)`
        IF `I am an employee` AND `it involves a breach of industrial law`
            THEN `the industrial commission (s 48); a union entitled to represent you may apply with your consent, and so may the Crime and Corruption Commission if you are a public officer and it may investigate the reprisal; no other court or tribunal but the Industrial Court has jurisdiction (s 48(2), (4))`
        OTHERWISE `the Supreme Court, because you can not apply to the industrial commission (s 49); the Crime and Corruption Commission may apply with your consent if you are a public officer and it may investigate the reprisal (s 49(2)(b))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `s 65 -- can my identity be revealed`

-- s 65(1): "If a person gains confidential information because of the person's involvement in this Act's
-- administration, the person must not make a record of the information, or intentionally or recklessly disclose the
-- information to anyone, other than under subsection (3). Maximum penalty -- 84 penalty units." (3): "(a) for this Act;
-- or (b) to discharge a function under another Act including, for example, to investigate something disclosed by a
-- public interest disclosure; or (c) for a proceeding in a court or tribunal; or (d) if the person to whom the
-- confidential information relates consents in writing ...; or (e) if -- (i) the person can not reasonably obtain the
-- consent ...; and (ii) making the record or disclosing the information is unlikely to harm the interests of the person
-- ... and is reasonable in all the circumstances; or (f) if the person reasonably believes that making the record or
-- disclosing the information is necessary to provide for the safety or welfare of a person; or (g) if authorised under a
-- regulation or another Act." (4), (5): natural justice, but for the discloser's identity "only if it is -- (a)
-- essential to do so under the principles of natural justice; and (b) unlikely a reprisal will be taken against the
-- person because of the disclosure." (7): confidential information includes the discloser's identity, occupation,
-- address and whereabouts. s 20(3)(c): a journalist told under s 20 does not gain it through the Act's administration.
DECLARE `Why they would reveal it` IS ONE OF
    `for the purposes of this Act`
    `to discharge a function under another Act, for example to investigate what I disclosed`
    `for a proceeding in a court or tribunal`
    `I consented in writing`
    `my consent could not reasonably be obtained, and revealing it is unlikely to harm me and is reasonable in all the circumstances`
    `they reasonably believe it is necessary for someone's safety or welfare`
    `a regulation or another Act authorises it`
    `natural justice to someone whose rights would otherwise be detrimentally affected`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Can my identity be revealed` IS ONE OF
    `yes: s 65(3) lets them record or disclose it for this purpose`
    `yes: natural justice may require it, and here it is essential under the principles of natural justice and a reprisal against you is unlikely (s 65(4), (5))`
    `no: your identity may be disclosed for natural justice only if that is essential and a reprisal against you is unlikely (s 65(5))`
    `no: none of the s 65(3) purposes applies; recording or disclosing it is an offence, maximum 84 penalty units (s 65(1)), and misconduct for a public officer (s 67)`
    `s 65 does not bind them: it binds only a person who gained the information through involvement in this Act's administration (s 65(1), (2)); a journalist you told under s 20 is not such a person (s 20(3)(c))`

@export Can a person who handled my public interest disclosure reveal my identity or record it?
GIVEN `they learned it through the Act's administration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person learned your identity because of their involvement in administering the Act (for example an officer who received or investigated your disclosure); answer no for a journalist you told under s 20
      `why` IS A `Why they would reveal it` @desc The reason for recording or revealing your identity
      `it is essential for natural justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for natural justice: whether revealing your identity is essential under the principles of natural justice
      `a reprisal is unlikely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for natural justice: whether it is unlikely a reprisal will be taken against you because of the disclosure
GIVETH A `Can my identity be revealed`
DECIDE `Can my identity be revealed question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `they learned it through the Act's administration`)
            THEN `s 65 does not bind them: it binds only a person who gained the information through involvement in this Act's administration (s 65(1), (2)); a journalist you told under s 20 is not such a person (s 20(3)(c))`
        IF `why` EQUALS `none of these`
            THEN `no: none of the s 65(3) purposes applies; recording or disclosing it is an offence, maximum 84 penalty units (s 65(1)), and misconduct for a public officer (s 67)`
        IF `why` EQUALS `natural justice to someone whose rights would otherwise be detrimentally affected`
            THEN IF `it is essential for natural justice` AND `a reprisal is unlikely`
                 THEN `yes: natural justice may require it, and here it is essential under the principles of natural justice and a reprisal against you is unlikely (s 65(4), (5))`
                 ELSE `no: your identity may be disclosed for natural justice only if that is essential and a reprisal against you is unlikely (s 65(5))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: s 65(3) lets them record or disclose it for this purpose`
